{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts that anthropogenic heat emissions are the single best explanation for the nocturnal UHI temperature gap and its cross-city variation, but this is an attribution error. The dominant, well-established mechanism sustaining the *classic* nocturnal UHI on a clear, calm night is the differential release of heat stored in urban thermal mass (impervious surfaces, building materials) during the day, combined with reduced radiative cooling due to low sky-view factor in urban canyons. While anthropogenic heat flux contributes to the urban energy balance, surface heat storage release is typically the largest nocturnal term. The target's own countercondition concedes this ambiguity, noting that 'high anthropogenic heat but also very high thermal mass and low sky view' confounds attribution, which contradicts its claim that anthropogenic heat is the 'single mechanism best explaining' the gap.","premise":"The established primary mechanism for the classic nocturnal UHI on clear, calm nights is the release of daytime heat stored in urban thermal mass and reduced longwave radiative cooling due to low sky-view factors, not anthropogenic heat emissions.","target":"ba67562331e4cc3cfd3af83987bacb03f28551efa5d1cf9e1e2f5383dfb4cd30"}]}